{
  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_003 (multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) achieved 0.619 accuracy, a ~9% jump over the singleprofile baseline (config_008, 0.527), confirming that multiprofile aggregation with avgpool is a critical design choice. The remaining untested variable most likely to yield a comparable gain is the image encoder: ResNet50 is a mid-tier backbone, while ImageNet-pretrained ViT-L is substantially more expressive. Question: Does upgrading the image encoder from ResNet50 to ViT-L improve validation accuracy when all other settings (multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool, concat) are held constant at their best-known values? This is a clean single-variable ablation of the image encoder. Why this is the best next experiment: With 7 experiments remaining, isolating the image-encoder effect on the current best architecture gives the highest information gain per experiment. If ViT-L helps, it establishes a new ceiling and guides subsequent experiments (e.g., pairing ViT-L with weighted fusion or transformer aggregation). If it does not help, it rules out the image encoder as a bottleneck and redirects effort toward RNA encoders or fusion strategies. Expected relative cost: moderately higher than config_003 (~36 GPU hrs) due to the larger ViT-L encoder, estimated around 50–70 GPU hrs, but justified because this configuration is the most likely candidate to become the best-performing model in the search."
}

